{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794425451,"nonce":"elzvJbbY4UfC7BuUYXXwLgxF3Yc=","title":"Owner decision needed. Raised 2026-11-12 by the devops-gitops pilot on 0.3.2, after hitting the build limits filed beside this ticket (cache, build args, ECR, `build.image` rendering). Analysis from the code; nothing run.","message":"Question: where should image building live? (a) a core Step kind, as today; (b) a first-party library through `invoke` (ADR-0076); (c) a plugin (ADR-0072).\n\nRecommendation: (b), with core keeping two small primitives. Not (c).\n\nWhy not (c), a plugin:\n- ADR-0076 §8 dropped `plugin_action` and `offer_actions`. A plugin today resolves gates; it does not run work inside a Run.\n- A plugin is one long-lived Pod per org in `scarab-plugins` under the `restricted` Pod Security level, with egress only to the CredentialBroker (no DNS, no registries, no S3, no Depot). A build needs the Run's workspace, the base-image registries, the cache store and the push registry. All of that would have to go through broker request rules written for the OCI distribution API.\n- A build is per Attempt and must be fenced, retried and logged as a Step. A plugin would rebuild all of that.\n\nWhy (b), a library:\n- Precedent: `clone` left core the same way (ADR-0076 §9, `libs/clone.yaml`), for the same reasons: a BuildKit or buildctl fix, or a new option (cache, build args, secrets, platforms, SBOM, attestations), ships as a library release, not a server release.\n- Today the BuildKit version is not pinned by the release at all: `BUILDKIT_IMAGE = \"moby/buildkit:rootless\"` (executor-k8s lib.rs:5675) is a floating tag. A library pins it by digest, as ADR-0076 §2 asks.\n- The `image` result keeps working: the library step writes buildctl's `--metadata-file` to `/scarab/results/image.json`, which is what the executor does now (lib.rs:5721).\n- Nothing is lost on fencing: `push_fence` and `image_artifact` (lib.rs:5769-5780) are called only from a test. The effective idempotency is the registry's: pushing the same digest twice is one effect; moving a tag is at-least-once under any option.\n- Cache auth is the same under every option: S3 through the Pod's IRSA, a registry cache through the mounted DOCKER_CONFIG.\n\nWhat core keeps (needed by (b); (a) has them built in):\n1. A build sandbox. Rootless BuildKit needs seccomp Unconfined, AppArmor unconfined, `allowPrivilegeEscalation: true`, uid 1000, no capabilities (`build_security_context`, lib.rs:5732, and the AppArmor annotation at lib.rs:4797). Today a `build:` step gets it with no grant because its command is fixed. A library step sets its own command, so the sandbox needs governing. Options:\n   - an ADR-0039 grant per Environment: wrong fit, because ordinary PR CI has no Environment and could no longer build;\n   - a new named sandbox (e.g. `security: { sandbox: rootless-buildkit }`) admitted only for an operator-approved image digest; or\n   - the same, admitted by a Policy over `origin` (ADR-0076 §1, ADR-0075 `step.admit`), so only Steps that come from the installed `lib:thulasi-ram/build` get it. Installed libraries are closed (they cannot `invoke file:`) and their inputs are typed, so a repository cannot swap the command. This is the closest to today's posture. Recommended.\n2. Registry credential delivery as a Step field, like `forge_token`: mount a dockerconfigjson at launch from the scoped `REGISTRY_AUTH` secret, the forge-derived GHCR / Forgejo credential, or an ECR source (see the ECR ticket). Never env.\n\nWhat (b) costs:\n- A migration for every `build:` pipeline (or the compiler lowers `build:` to `invoke: lib:thulasi-ram/build` for a while).\n- ADR-0018's \"secure by default for the most common job\" moves from a compile-time check to a library plus a Policy. The first-party library is offered by default (ADR-0076 §5), so the authoring effort stays low.\n- Needs an ADR amending 0018 (owner's call).\n\nEffect on the filed tickets: the ECR credential, changed paths and `build.image` rendering are core under any option. Cache and build args become library inputs under (b), so hold them until this is decided.\n\nWorkaround the pilot can use now, with no Scarab change: an Environment admin grants `privileged` (ADR-0039) for a digest-pinned BuildKit image, and a normal step runs buildctl with any flags. Runs only in Runs with an Environment target; privileged is node-level power for that step, and the admin re-approves each digest. Unverified on EKS. The `k8s_overlay` route described in ADR-0055 (\"strategic-merged\") does not match the code: `json_merge` (lib.rs:5168) is RFC-7386, so an overlay on `spec.containers` replaces the whole list.","files":null}]}